"""TEMPLATE — copy to <workflow>/<agent_token>_validate.py and fill in.

`.template` suffix so pytest collection, globs, and imports skip it. (The
previous placeholder was a real `.py` file named `{agent_name}_validate.py`,
which is not an importable module name and trips any collector that walks
this tree.)

THE CUSTOM PRECHECK HOOK — the narrow structural checks the config-driven
`precheck` block in the rubric YAML cannot express: cross-referencing two
sections of a document, parsing a structure, verifying specific values.

Referenced from the rubric by path relative to it:

    validate: ./your_agent_validate.py:check

Scope discipline, in order of importance:

1. STRUCTURAL ONLY. If a check requires judgement about whether the content
   is *good*, it belongs in the judge rubric. A precheck that guesses at
   quality produces false failures, and a false failure is worse than no
   check — it makes the pass rate meaningless and hides real regressions.
2. NO FALSE POSITIVES. This gate decides whether a response is judged at all.
   Prefer missing a real defect to rejecting a valid response.
3. NAME THE DEFECT. The reason string is what a human reads when a run fails.
   "3 nav target(s) with no matching page section: ['#/invoices', ...]" is
   actionable; "structure invalid" is not.

Keep it to one `check` function. If it needs helpers, they are private.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import re

_EXAMPLE_RE = re.compile(r"^###\s+(.+)$", re.MULTILINE)


def check(response: str) -> tuple[bool, str]:
    """Verify <the one thing this checks>.

    Returns (passed, reason). The reason is recorded on both pass and fail —
    on pass it says what was verified, which is how you confirm the check is
    actually running and not silently matching nothing.
    """
    headings = _EXAMPLE_RE.findall(response)

    # Guard the "matched nothing" case explicitly. A check that finds no
    # input to check must FAIL, never silently pass — this is the single most
    # common way a precheck stops doing anything without anyone noticing.
    if not headings:
        return False, "no '### ' section headings found"

    return True, f"all {len(headings)} section(s) well-formed"
